    "turn_idx": 5,
    "turn_text": "It was heavily skewed towards a no vote. In the middle of that on the front end, I do listen. We clearly pay attention, but there's so much minutiae going on, and there's so many backstories and back alley pieces of information and whatnot. You can only process so much of that. But it was heavily skewed towards a no vote. Ultimately, I did go, no, which was not an easy thing to do. Frankly, we were We were trying to get back to as close as possible to the bill that we actually passed. The bill that we passed out of the house was a good bill. And it shifted the paradigm on spending. It had some very significant cuts and it had a hard lid on it in terms of the increase of the ceiling, of the debt ceiling. And that's really the main thing that I wanted. It also got rid of the IRS army and the whole student loan nonsense. or the payoff of the student loan, which to me is nonsense. And we lost that in that negotiation. I'm not throwing Kevin or Garrett or Patrick under the bus. They're one-third out of a three-way negotiation. It's house versus sand versus white house. And they probably came out of that with the best deal that they could have under the circumstances. I don't think it was suitable for me and I don't think it was suitable for the state of Idaho and we didn't get enough of those critical components for me to be able to come home and say, yeah, here's what I did and I'm proud of it because I wouldn't have been.",
