    "turn_idx": 14,
    "turn_text": "Well, I think fundamentally with those of us who are more on the fiscal conservative front, it tends to reinforce the government as usual type thing. We just can't break the addiction, right? It's the addiction to the spending and the inability to just say no when sometimes you just got to say no. And okay, if 32 trillion isn't enough in debt to say no is 33, is 40? is 50, you know, I mean, because we're kind of at the point where realistically Nate, you and I are going to be six feet under by the time, you know, this has any realistic chance to get paid off. And so, you know, I think it reinforces the government as usual, which is really unfortunate, and that's also one of the reasons why I was logging in the OT trying to do something to get this to a point where I could say, yeah, this is really worth going after. And now, frankly, I think it's going to go through as is. I was talking with Senator Lee. He's likely going to take a right on amendment over there. But Schumer, I think, is going to rally enough Democrat votes behind it that it's probably going to pass as is if it doesn't. uh... you know it's it's uh... uh... you know we'll we'll turn on a senate amendment and try to get that turned around because supposedly we turn into a pumpkin on june great june five i think it is is that yeah is when the uh... but when the lid uh... is exceeded and we we uh... officially default and so nobody wants to do that but uh... we were reinforcing that the the bad government think stereotype and that's not a good thing. However, I don't want to downplay this so bad. This is not a disaster in the sense of overall because uh... it's better than where we are right now it's just not anywhere at work close to work i'd hope we would be",
